  "slug": "untitled-topic-3bda",
  "summary": "Single-nucleus, chromatin and long-read profiling now resolve stromal states and matrix-remodelling cores across human tissues and disease.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is how stromal cells are defined by their states and by the matrix-remodelling programmes they carry. In human atrial fibrillation, single-nucleus multi-omics identifies fibroblast states and implicates JMJD1C in TGF-beta-driven fibroblast remodelling. In healthy synovium and infrapatellar fat pad, paired bulk, single-nucleus RNA and chromatin accessibility profiling describes a continuous stromal continuum rather than discrete populations. Cross-organ analysis of human and mouse fibrosis RNA-seq data identifies a conserved 25-gene matrix-remodelling core, although gene-level cross-species concordance is weak. Human stromal clones with high bone-forming capacity share HIPPO competency signatures and oxidative metabolism. What is new is the combination of single-nucleus and chromatin-level profiling with cross-organ comparison, while the conserved core remains weakly concordant between species. The heart isoform atlas and bovine MSC culture transcriptomics address adjacent questions and do not directly examine stromal matrix control.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
